    "result_text": "Done. I agreed with the finding and fixed it.\n\n**Change made:** `getOwnerSegment()` no longer borrows the postcode-validation predicate `hasPostcodeRange` for its AREA decision. Instead I added an intent-revealing predicate:\n\n```java\npublic static boolean isKnownRegion(String region) {\n    return !\"UNKNOWN\".equals(region);\n}\n```\n\nand call `isKnownRegion(getLocality())`.\n\n**Why this backing rather than a `{NSW, VIC, QLD}` set:** the domain already defines the canonical notion directly \u2014 `getLocality()`/`identityRegion()` yield a real region or the `\"UNKNOWN\"` sentinel, and `regionForCity()` uses the same sentinel. So \"known region\" is precisely \"not the UNKNOWN sentinel\". This decouples segment logic from *all three* coincidentally-keyed tables (`CITY_REGION`, `REGION_POSTCODE_RANGES`, `REGION_TIMEZONE`) rather than swapping one accidental coupling for another, and it avoids hard-coding a fourth duplicate of the region names. The predicate sits next to `identityRegion`, whose sentinel it interprets, with Javadoc in house style.\n\nAcceptance behaviour is unchanged: NSW/VIC/QLD \u2192 `METRO`, `UNKNOWN` \u2192 `REGIONAL`. Full suite is green (2/2, `BUILD SUCCESS`).",
